Claude firestore is a server list no  sequel document database that simplifies  storing syncing and querying data for  your mobile and web apps at global scale  you may have heard the cloud fire store  is the next generation of cloud  datastore but what does that mean what  makes it different and what should you  do if you're an existing data store  customer  I'm Kevin Nelson find out answers to all  those questions and more in this video  let's get the question I always get  asked first  out of the way I want to start off by  assuring datastore users that all your  existing datastore applications will  continue to work under cloud fire store  running in datastore mode you won't have  to make any code changes and you'll  experience zero downtime when your  service is automatically upgraded after  cloud fire store becomes generally  available so with that out of the way  let's do a quick review of what  datastore is and then I'll get into the  new capabilities being introduced in  cloud fire store Google Cloud datastore  is a fully managed no sequel document  database built for automatic scaling  high performance and ease of application  development it was introduced with  Google App Engine Google's original  serverless development platform that  today handles the toughest workloads  from some of the world's most popular  apps cloud fire store builds on the  features and massive scalability of  datastore while adding several important  new features the first thing to  understand is that cloud firestar runs  in two mutually exclusive modes  datastore mode and native mode in  datastore mode cloud fire store runs all  your existing datastore applications and  eliminates some of datastores  restrictions cloud fire store is built  on an entirely new back-end that brings  the best of Google clouds powerful cloud  infrastructure to existing datastore  applications this includes strong  consistency automatic multi-region data  replication atomic batch operations and  transaction support perhaps even better  cloud forest or completely eliminates  the entity group transaction limits that  were present in datastore cloud fire  store has no entity groups and therefore  no entity group limits  you get all of these new capabilities  and compatibility with datastore when  running cloud fire store in datastore  mode cloud fire store native mode has  all the advantages of datastore mode but  also contains a number of new features  that make it easier to power native  mobile and web apps including robust  multi device synchronization full  offline support and a comprehensive set  of client libraries which support an  integrated storage solution across  Google App Engine kubernetes and mobile  devices you can learn more about this  using the link to my left it's important  to understand though that cloud fire  store native mode isn't compatible with  existing data store applications you'll  have to setup a new project with a new  cloud fire store database if you want to  use native mode in either mode cloud  fire store is a truly serverless  database service that eliminates the  need to think about spinning up virtual  machines data replication or scaling  should your app hit the first page of  tech name and suddenly grow from  hundreds of QPS  to millions cloud fire store will  automatically scale to handle that surge  in traffic and congratulations the data  model and cloud fire store native mode  is similar to the data store data model  but there are a few differences data in  cloud fire stores organized into  documents and collections which are  similar to entities and kinds in  datastore  at the highest level a cloud fire store  database can have any number of  collections which contain any number of  documents a document contains a group of  key value pairs called fields in fire  store similar to properties in datastore  documents can also contain sub  collections of additional documents  notice the alternating pattern of  collections and documents your  collections and documents must always  follow this pattern  you cannot reference a collection in a  collection or a document within a  document  managing indexes in cloud fire store is  also a little different than how they're  managed in datastore like datastore  cloud fire store requires an index for  every query fire store automatically  creates an index for every document  field  compound queries require that you first  create a composite index that maps to  the range clause of your query these are  easy to manage directly from the  firestore console you'll even be  prompted to create the composite index  the first time you attempt to query that  doesn't have an existing index when you  create a new cloud firestore database  you'll select which mode you want either  datastore mode or firestore native mode  consider this choice carefully because  you won't be able to change the mode  once the database has been created as I  mentioned earlier to take full advantage  of all the new capabilities of cloud  firestorm like offline support and  device synchronization existing data  store users will need to set up a new  project with a new database in fire  store native mode this was a very basic  introduction to the cloud fire store  data model you can learn more in my  colleague Todd Kerpelman video series  gets a no cloud fire store on the Google  cloud YouTube channel and online at  cloud google.com slash fire store there  you can learn all about cloud fire  stores schema-less data model and  advanced query capabilities ok so now  you're probably asking yourself fire  store sounds great but what does it  really mean for my existing application  using data store great question  during beta data store customers or  really anyone can try out fire store by  creating a fire store database in a new  project select fire store under the  storage options in the GCP console if  you want to move existing data into fire  store you can do so using the managed  export and import service once cloud  fire store has been launched for general  availability all data store projects  will be automatically migrated to fire  store running in data store mode the  upgrade will be done automatically for  you with no downtime and no changes to  your application absolutely no action is  required on your part migrating all data  store customers will likely take a few  months you'll be notified when your  migration is scheduled to begin if that  time isn't convenient or you have a  critical time window where you don't  want your application to possibly be  impacted by the automatic migration say  Black Friday you'll be able to indicate  preference  via Google form but again you shouldn't  notice any differences at all during or  after migration so now you may be  wondering whether your next project  should be built using cloud firestore in  datastore mode or native mode in general  we recommend building new applications  in native mode so your app can take  advantage of all fire source  capabilities but keep in mind that  during the beta period cloud fires to  our won't have an SLA and cloud fire  store in native mode may have some  restrictions on the number of concurrent  connections and the maximum number of  writes per second those caps may rise as  we get closer to GA refer to the  documentation for the latest details  so cloud fire store in native mode lets  you take advantage of fire stores full  capabilities but may not be appropriate  for large mission-critical enterprise  applications until cloud fire store goes  GA so to recap cloud fire store is the  next generation of data store all  existing data store databases will be  migrated to cloud fire store running in  data store mode beginning shortly after  general availability that migration will  be automatic and seamless fire store in  native mode adds powerful new  capabilities for web and mobile  developers like comprehensive client  libraries offline support and automatic  device synchronization I hope this video  has given you a better idea about some  of the new capabilities of fire store  and what it means for your existing data  store applications you can find lots  more information about fire store on the  Google cloud youtube channel and in the  online docs you'll find those links  right next to me thanks for listening  [Music]
 

